      "id": 1,
      "text": "Elgin grew into a rail hub by 1870, with tracks crisscrossing the town and spurring rapid industrial growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://elginhistory.org/hours-and-location/exhibits-overview/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Fox River was the reason James Gifford founded Elgin in 1835, providing water power for early mills and factories.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://elginhistory.org/hours-and-location/exhibits-overview/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Elgin’s historic Rugby Colony, founded in 1880 by author Thomas Hughes, is a restored Victorian village listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://cumberlandnationalscenicbyway.com/morgan-county/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Rugby Colony features Folk Victorian buildings, including a library, school, church, and the Tabard Inn, all built in the late 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://cumberlandnationalscenicbyway.com/morgan-county/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Morgan County, where Elgin sits, was created in 1817 and named after Revolutionary War hero Daniel Morgan, who won at Cowpens.",
